1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,581
This is ECE 358, video 15b. The topic is DHCP, the protocol that gives a host its IP address.

2
00:00:11,031 --> 00:00:27,394
After this video, you can explain the four DHCP messages, with their addresses and ports. You can name the four parameters that DHCP gives a client. Then we solve questions 48 to 56 of the question bank.

3
00:00:27,844 --> 00:00:41,540
We now know how to split a block into subnets. But how does a device get an IP address, for example 223.1.2.4/24? That is two questions.

4
00:00:41,990 --> 00:00:52,017
The first question: how does a network get the network part of its addresses? The ISP gives the block. We answer the rest at the end of this video.

5
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:59,592
The second question: how does a host get an IP address inside its network, that is, the host part?

6
00:01:00,042 --> 00:01:05,546
One way: a system administrator hard-codes the address in a configuration file.

7
00:01:05,996 --> 00:01:16,599
The other way: DHCP, the dynamic host configuration protocol. The host gets an address from a server when it connects. It is plug-and-play.

8
00:01:17,049 --> 00:01:33,582
DHCP has this goal: a host gets its IP address from a network server when it joins the network. The server has a pool of addresses for the subnet 223.1.2.0/24.

9
00:01:34,032 --> 00:01:39,237
Here, host A joins. The server gives it the address .4 from its pool.

10
00:01:39,687 --> 00:01:46,578
The address comes with a lease. A lease is a lifetime, for example 3600 seconds.

11
00:01:47,028 --> 00:01:51,913
Before the lease ends, the host can renew the lease. Then it keeps the same address.

12
00:01:52,363 --> 00:01:57,761
When host A leaves, its lease ends. The address .4 goes back to the pool.

13
00:01:58,211 --> 00:02:03,437
So addresses are reused: a host holds an address only while it is connected.

14
00:02:03,887 --> 00:02:08,943
Later, a mobile host B joins, and it gets the same address .4.

15
00:02:09,393 --> 00:02:14,449
So DHCP supports mobile users who join and leave the network.

16
00:02:14,899 --> 00:02:24,073
Here is the DHCP scenario of slide 42. It is the network of slide 29: one router and three subnets.

17
00:02:24,523 --> 00:02:31,051
The DHCP server has the address 223.1.2.5.

18
00:02:31,501 --> 00:02:39,458
Typically, the DHCP server is co-located in the router. Then it serves all subnets that connect to the router.

19
00:02:39,908 --> 00:02:53,497
A new client arrives in the subnet 223.1.2.0/24. It has no IP address yet. It must get one from the DHCP server.

20
00:02:53,947 --> 00:03:06,342
DHCP uses four types of messages. First, the client broadcasts a DHCP discover message. It asks: is there a DHCP server out there?

21
00:03:06,792 --> 00:03:15,112
The source address is 0.0.0.0, port 68, because the client has no address yet.

22
00:03:15,562 --> 00:03:27,615
The destination is the broadcast address 255.255.255.255, port 67.

23
00:03:28,065 --> 00:03:31,521
The transaction ID is 654.

24
00:03:31,971 --> 00:03:39,395
Second, the server answers with a DHCP offer. It says: here is an IP address that you can use.

25
00:03:39,845 --> 00:03:46,416
The source is the server, 223.1.2.5, port 67.

26
00:03:46,866 --> 00:03:55,442
The destination is again the broadcast address, port 68. The client cannot receive at an address that it does not have yet.

27
00:03:55,892 --> 00:04:05,855
The field yiaddr means your IP address. It holds the offered address, 223.1.2.4.

28
00:04:06,305 --> 00:04:10,657
The lifetime of the address is 3600 seconds.

29
00:04:11,107 --> 00:04:19,789
The offer uses the same transaction ID, 654. So the client can match the offer to its discover.

30
00:04:20,239 --> 00:04:27,109
Third, the client sends a DHCP request. It says: I would like to use this IP address.

31
00:04:27,559 --> 00:04:43,644
It is still a broadcast, from 0.0.0.0, port 68, to 255.255.255.255, port 67.

32
00:04:44,094 --> 00:04:49,513
It names the offered address, 223.1.2.4.

33
00:04:49,963 --> 00:04:54,101
It has a new transaction ID, 655.

34
00:04:54,551 --> 00:05:00,610
Fourth, the server sends a DHCP ACK. It says: you have got that IP address.

35
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:10,596
After the ACK, the client uses 223.1.2.4 for the lifetime of 3600 seconds.

36
00:05:11,046 --> 00:05:26,150
The discover and the offer can be skipped. RFC 2131 says that a client can skip them if it remembers a previous address and wants to use it again. The request and the ACK are always used.

37
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:35,261
Look at the discover message as one datagram. It has an IP header, a UDP header, and the DHCP message.

38
00:05:35,711 --> 00:05:44,650
In the IP header, the source address is 0.0.0.0. The client has no IP address yet.

39
00:05:45,100 --> 00:06:02,444
The destination address is 255.255.255.255, the broadcast address. The client does not know the address of the server, so it sends to all nodes on its subnet.

40
00:06:02,894 --> 00:06:09,785
DHCP runs over UDP. The client sends from UDP port 68.

41
00:06:10,235 --> 00:06:16,805
The server listens on UDP port 67. So the destination port is 67.

42
00:06:17,255 --> 00:06:24,551
In the DHCP message, yiaddr is still 0.0.0.0.

43
00:06:25,001 --> 00:06:36,095
The transaction ID is 654. The server copies the transaction ID into its offer. So the client can match the offer to its discover.

44
00:06:36,545 --> 00:06:51,755
DHCP can return more than the allocated address. The slide lists four key pieces of information. The first is the IP address of the client, here 223.1.2.4.

45
00:06:52,205 --> 00:07:03,768
The second is the IP address of the first-hop router for the client. In this network, it is the router interface 223.1.2.9.

46
00:07:04,218 --> 00:07:09,146
The third is the name and the IP address of the local DNS server.

47
00:07:09,596 --> 00:07:28,497
The fourth is the network mask. It shows which part of the address is the network part. For the subnet 223.1.2.0/24, the mask is 255.255.255.0.

48
00:07:28,947 --> 00:07:38,739
With its own IP address and the mask, the client can compute the broadcast address of its network. Write the address and the mask in binary.

49
00:07:39,189 --> 00:07:45,952
Keep the bits where the mask is 1. They are the network part, 223.1.2.

50
00:07:46,402 --> 00:07:56,813
Set the bits where the mask is 0 to 1. The result is 223.1.2.255, the broadcast address.

51
00:07:57,263 --> 00:08:11,172
Here is the example of slides 46 and 47. A laptop connects to a network. It needs its IP address, the address of its first-hop router, and the address of its DNS server.

52
00:08:11,622 --> 00:08:14,971
The laptop makes a DHCP request message.

53
00:08:15,421 --> 00:08:25,960
The DHCP request is encapsulated in UDP, then in IP, then in an Ethernet frame. This is the layering from chapter 1.

54
00:08:26,410 --> 00:08:33,642
The Ethernet frame is broadcast on the LAN. Its destination MAC address is all ones, F F F F F F F F F F F F.

55
00:08:34,092 --> 00:08:39,169
The router runs the DHCP server. It receives the broadcast frame.

56
00:08:39,619 --> 00:08:50,926
The router demultiplexes the frame. Ethernet passes it up to IP, IP passes it up to UDP, and UDP passes it up to DHCP.

57
00:08:51,376 --> 00:09:05,477
The DHCP server makes a DHCP ACK. It contains the client's IP address, the IP address of the first-hop router, and the name and IP address of the DNS server.

58
00:09:05,927 --> 00:09:15,741
The ACK is encapsulated and sent to the client, and it goes up to DHCP in the client. Now the client knows all of these addresses.

59
00:09:16,191 --> 00:09:24,809
How does an ISP get a block of IP addresses? From ICANN, the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers.

60
00:09:25,259 --> 00:09:35,201
ICANN also manages the DNS root zone. This includes the delegation of the management of top-level domains, such as dot com.

61
00:09:35,651 --> 00:09:45,528
ICANN allocates IP addresses through 5 regional registries. The regional registries can then allocate addresses to local registries.

62
00:09:45,978 --> 00:09:58,842
Are there enough 32-bit IP addresses? 2 to the power 32 is about 4.3 times 10 to the power 9. That is about 4.3 billion addresses.

63
00:09:59,292 --> 00:10:08,295
That is not enough. ICANN allocated the last chunk of IPv4 addresses to the regional registries in 2011.

64
00:10:08,745 --> 00:10:15,294
NAT, the topic of a later video, helps with the exhaustion of the IPv4 address space.

65
00:10:15,744 --> 00:10:27,947
IPv6 has a 128-bit address space. 2 to the power 128 is about 3.4 times 10 to the power 38 addresses.

66
00:10:28,397 --> 00:10:41,495
Now the DHCP questions. Question 48: at power on, a laptop wants an IP address. Which application layer protocol does it run? Pause the video and try it.

67
00:10:41,945 --> 00:10:43,972
The answer is DHCP.

68
00:10:44,422 --> 00:10:54,961
Question 49: name the four parameters that a DHCP client gets: three IP addresses and one mask. Pause the video and try it.

69
00:10:55,411 --> 00:11:06,013
The client's own IP address, the IP address of the first-hop router, the IP address of the local DNS server, and the network mask.

70
00:11:06,463 --> 00:11:17,941
Questions 50 and 51: name the four message types. Name the two port numbers. Are they TCP or UDP ports? Pause the video and try it.

71
00:11:18,391 --> 00:11:22,295
The four messages are discover, offer, request, and ACK.

72
00:11:22,745 --> 00:11:31,470
DHCP uses UDP. The server uses port 67, and the client uses port 68.

73
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:47,707
Question 52: why does DHCP use UDP, rather than TCP? Question 53: which IP broadcast address does the client use to reach a DHCP server? Pause the video and try it.

74
00:11:48,157 --> 00:11:57,650
TCP is connection-oriented. Before it sends data, it must set up a connection with a three-way handshake to a known IP address.

75
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:04,820
But a new DHCP client has no IP address, and it does not know the address of the server.

76
00:12:05,270 --> 00:12:17,686
UDP needs no connection. The client can send one UDP datagram to the broadcast address. Every node on the subnet receives it, also the DHCP server.

77
00:12:18,136 --> 00:12:31,491
For question 53, the client sends to the IP broadcast address 255.255.255.255.

78
00:12:31,941 --> 00:12:40,154
Question 54: why does a dynamic address have a definite lease, for example one hour? Pause the video and try it.

79
00:12:40,604 --> 00:12:49,713
The lease allows addresses to be reused. A host holds an address only while it is connected. If it stays, it renews the lease.

80
00:12:50,163 --> 00:13:00,510
Question 55: the server offers an address. Why does the client not use it as the source address of its request? Pause the video and try it.

81
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:15,744
Because the address is only an offer. More than one server can make an offer. The client may use an address only after the server confirms it with the ACK. So the source stays 0.0.0.0.

82
00:13:16,194 --> 00:13:27,693
Question 56: the client knows the server address from the offer. Why does it broadcast the request, and not send it only to that server? Pause the video and try it.

83
00:13:28,143 --> 00:13:36,292
First, the client still has no address of its own. Its source address is still 0.0.0.0.

84
00:13:36,742 --> 00:13:45,873
Second, the broadcast tells every DHCP server which offer the client takes. The other servers can then keep their offered addresses.

85
00:13:46,323 --> 00:13:53,235
These two reasons come from RFC 2131, which slide 43 cites.

86
00:13:53,685 --> 00:14:09,706
Here is the recap. A host gets its IP address in one of two ways: a system administrator hard-codes it, or the host uses DHCP. DHCP gives an address with a lease, so addresses are reused.

87
00:14:10,156 --> 00:14:34,156
DHCP uses four messages: discover, offer, request and ACK. They go over UDP, server port 67 and client port 68, from 0.0.0.0 to the broadcast address 255.255.255.255.

88
00:14:34,606 --> 00:14:43,289
DHCP gives the client its own IP address, its first-hop router, its DNS server and its network mask.

89
00:14:43,739 --> 00:14:59,824
ISPs get their blocks from ICANN through five regional registries. IPv4 addresses ran out in 2011. NAT and IPv6 help. The next video covers forwarding and address aggregation.
